---
/**
 * how-it-works.astro — Plain-language explainer of the current council system.
 * Rebuilt from design-concepts/how-it-works.html. Static article (shared CSS).
 */
import Layout from "../components/Layout.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const b = base.endsWith("/") ? base : base + "/";
---
<Layout
  title="How Columbus City Council works"
  description="A plain-language explainer of how Columbus City Council is structured — nine district members elected citywide under the current hybrid system."
  active="how"
>
  <div class="wrap"><div class="article">
    <section class="art-hero">
      <div class="eyebrow">The basics</div>
      <h1>How Columbus City Council works</h1>
      <p class="dek">Columbus uses a system that trips up even lifelong residents. Here's the short version: nine members, each living in a different district &mdash; but for now, every voter in the city helps elect all nine.</p>
      <div class="keyfacts">
        <div class="kf"><div class="kfl">Seats</div><div class="kfv">9 council members</div></div>
        <div class="kf"><div class="kfl">Districts</div><div class="kfv">~100,000 residents each</div></div>
        <div class="kf"><div class="kfl">Term</div><div class="kfv">4 years, staggered</div></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <h2>The part that confuses people</h2>
    <p>Columbus has a <strong>hybrid</strong> system. Council members must <strong>live in the district they represent</strong> &mdash; but every Columbus voter <strong>votes in all nine district races</strong>, not just their own. So your ballot includes all nine seats, and every member is ultimately accountable to the whole city.</p>
    <div class="vote-cta" style="margin:1.4rem 0;">
      <div class="vc"><div class="vk">Step 1 &middot; Live</div><div class="vv">Members live local</div><p style="font-size:.9rem;color:var(--ink-2);margin:0;">Each candidate must reside in the district they want to represent.</p></div>
      <div class="vc"><div class="vk">Step 2 &middot; Vote</div><div class="vv">Everyone votes citywide</div><p style="font-size:.9rem;color:var(--ink-2);margin:0;">All Columbus voters weigh in on all nine seats.</p></div>
      <div class="vc"><div class="vk">Step 3 &middot; Serve</div><div class="vv">Answerable to the city</div><p style="font-size:.9rem;color:var(--ink-2);margin:0;">Members represent a district but answer to all of Columbus.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="callout"><b>This may change in November.</b> In 2026, Columbus voters are expected to weigh two charter amendments on how council is elected &mdash; a move to single-member districts and a proposal to expand the council to 13 members. <a href={`${b}2026-ballot/`}>Read the ballot explainer &rarr;</a></div>

    <h2>How we got here</h2>
    <div class="timeline">
      <div class="tl"><div class="tdate">1914&ndash;2023</div><div class="ty">A century at-large</div><div class="td">For more than 100 years, Columbus elected all council members citywide, with no districts at all.</div></div>
      <div class="tl"><div class="tdate">2018</div><div class="ty">Voters approve districts</div><div class="td">Columbus voters amend the city charter to create residential districts and expand council from seven seats to nine.</div></div>
      <div class="tl"><div class="tdate">2021</div><div class="ty">The map is drawn</div><div class="td">A districting commission develops map options; City Council selects the final nine-district map in December.</div></div>
      <div class="tl"><div class="tdate">2023</div><div class="ty">The system begins</div><div class="td">All nine district seats are filled for the first time.</div></div>
      <div class="tl now"><div class="tdate">2024</div><div class="ty">Terms get staggered</div><div class="td">Members draw lots: four take two-year terms (up in 2025), five take four-year terms (up in 2027). All future terms are four years, staggered so the whole council never turns over at once.</div></div>
    </div>

    <h2>What City Council actually does</h2>
    <p>Council is the legislative body of the City of Columbus. It <strong>adopts the city's operating and capital budgets</strong>, <strong>passes city ordinances</strong> (the Columbus City Codes), and <strong>approves city contracts</strong>. It's led by a Council President &mdash; currently Shannon G. Hardin. Day-to-day city operations are run by the Mayor's administration; Council sets policy and holds the purse strings.</p>

    <h2>How to get involved</h2>
    <div class="involve" style="margin-top:1rem;">
      <div class="icard"><svg class="ic" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><rect x="3" y="4" width="18" height="17" rx="2"/><path d="M3 9h18M8 2v4M16 2v4"/></svg><h3>Attend a meeting</h3><p>Council meets at City Hall and the sessions are open to the public, with time set aside for public comment.</p><a href="https://www.columbus.gov/Government/City-Council" target="_blank" rel="noopener">See the calendar &rarr;</a></div>
      <div class="icard"><svg class="ic" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M12 21s-6-5.3-6-10a6 6 0 0 1 12 0c0 4.7-6 10-6 10Z"/><circle cx="12" cy="11" r="2"/></svg><h3>Engage your area commission</h3><p>The most local level of input &mdash; area commissions review zoning and advise Council on neighborhood matters.</p><a href="https://cbusareacommissions.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Find yours &rarr;</a></div>
      <div class="icard"><svg class="ic" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M4 4h16v12H7l-3 3V4Z"/></svg><h3>Contact your members</h3><p>Because every member is elected citywide, you can reach any of the nine &mdash; not just the one in your district.</p><a href="https://www.columbus.gov/Government/City-Council/Directory" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Council directory &rarr;</a></div>
    </div>
  </div></div>
</Layout>
